{
  "dom_id": "step-q215406",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The point of intersection of the lines $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
  "worked_solution": "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=5x+2$ and $y=-2x-5.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=5x+2 \\\\ y=-2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=5x+2$ into the second equation $y=-2x-5$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2 & =-2x-5 \\\\ 5x & =-2x-7 \\\\ 7x & =-7 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=-1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=5x+2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5x+2 \\\\  & =5(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-5+2 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-1,\\,y=-3.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,-1,-3\\,).$",
